WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.257
Correlation is useful, but it can be misleading when we ignore the shape and context of the data.

00:00:06.257 --> 00:00:10.482
A curved relationship may have a low linear correlation.

00:00:10.482 --> 00:00:14.827
One extreme observation can change the coefficient dramatically.

00:00:14.827 --> 00:00:17.962
Subgroups can create or hide a pattern.

00:00:17.962 --> 00:00:21.798
Restricted ranges can also weaken an apparent relationship.

00:00:22.453 --> 00:00:26.625
This is why correlation should not be the first and only analysis.

00:00:26.625 --> 00:00:33.895
A better sequence is to plot the variables, inspect outliers and groups, calculate the coefficient,

00:00:33.895 --> 00:00:36.585
and then interpret it in context.

00:00:36.585 --> 00:00:45.285
When you see a correlation, ask what the scatter plot looks like and whether another variable could be affecting both

00:00:45.285 --> 00:00:50.789
measurements. Good statistical reasoning combines the number with the data-generating process.
